IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- scja-core.l4: the shared nouns of the Supreme Court of Judicature Act 1969 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition,
-- informal consolidation in force from 1 July 2024. The source is a plain-text rendering (SCJA1969.txt), not the official text.
-- DECLARE and the Act's own figures and dates only; no rule is written here. Imports prelude and daydate only.
-- A name is here if a witness of ordinary competence could testify to the fact; whether the fact has a legal effect is a part's rule.
-- Seat served: a person who wishes to start, defend or appeal a civil case in the High Court or the Court of Appeal.
-- Dates are written `Date day month year`. Names defined by the staged Interpretation Act modules are not redefined here (PLAN.md lists them).

§ `Core: courts`

-- src:381 | “court” means the General Division, the Appellate Division, or
-- src:382 | the Court of Appeal;
-- src:424 | “subordinate court” means a court constituted under the State
-- src:425 | Courts Act 1970, a Family Court or Youth Court constituted
-- src:426 | under the Family Justice Act 2014, and any other court,
-- src:427 | tribunal or judicial or quasi‑judicial body from the decisions
-- src:428 | of which under any written law there is a right of appeal to the
-- src:429 | Supreme Court;
@ref s 2 "court" and "subordinate court" (src:381-382) (src:424-429)
-- Which court made a decision, or is hearing a case. The Singapore International Commercial Court is a division of the General Division (s 18A),
-- and the Act sometimes counts it in "General Division" and sometimes not (s 18C, s 18J(4)): each part decides, per section, and records a fork.
DECLARE Court IS ONE OF
    `General Division`
    `Singapore International Commercial Court`
    `Family Division of the High Court`
    `Appellate Division`
    `Court of Appeal`
    `District Court`
    `Magistrate's Court`
    `Family Court`
    `Youth Court`
    `Syariah Court`
    `another subordinate court, tribunal or judicial or quasi-judicial body`

-- How a court is hearing a case (ss 8, 8A, 17B, 22A, 37, 55).
@ref ss 8, 8A, 17B, 22A, 37, 55
DECLARE `Manner of hearing` IS ONE OF
    `in open court`
    `in private`
    `through a live video link`
    `through a live television link`
    `through a live audio link only`
    `asynchronously, by exchange of written correspondence`
    `without hearing oral arguments`

-- What the Act says a case is exercising: original or appellate jurisdiction (ss 29, 29A, 29C, 35, 53), or supervision and revision (ss 23-28).
@ref ss 15-28
DECLARE `Jurisdiction exercised` IS ONE OF
    `original jurisdiction`
    `appellate jurisdiction`
    `supervisory and revisionary jurisdiction`

-- s 20 speaks of jurisdiction "of a quasi-criminal or civil nature"; ss 15 and 19 of criminal jurisdiction.
@ref ss 15, 19, 20
DECLARE `Nature of the proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `a civil proceeding`
    `a quasi-criminal proceeding`
    `a criminal proceeding`

§ `Core: Judges, panels and votes`

-- src:431 | “Supreme Court Judge” means the Chief Justice, a Justice of the
-- src:432 | Court of Appeal, a Judge of the Appellate Division or a Judge
-- src:433 | of the High Court.
@ref s 2 "Supreme Court Judge" and s 4 (src:431-433)
DECLARE `Kind of Judge` IS ONE OF
    `Chief Justice`
    `Vice-President of the Court of Appeal`
    `Justice of the Court of Appeal`
    `President of the Appellate Division`
    `Judge of the Appellate Division`
    `Judge of the High Court`
    `Judicial Commissioner`
    `Senior Judge`
    `International Judge`

-- Equality of `reference for this person` is identity. s 4 ranks Supreme Court Judges by office and then by "the order of their appointments".
@ref s 2 "Judge", s 4
DECLARE Judge HAS
    `reference for this person` IS A STRING
    office IS A `Kind of Judge`
    `date of appointment` IS A DATE

-- The Judges sitting in one court on one case.
@ref ss 10, 18G, 18H, 32, 33, 36, 50, 51, 54
DECLARE Bench HAS
    court IS A Court
    judges IS A LIST OF Judge

-- How the Judges of a bench divided: for the claim, appeal or application, and against it (ss 10(1B), 18H(3) and (6), 33, 51).
@ref ss 10(1B), 18H, 33, 51
DECLARE `Split of opinion` HAS
    `Judges for the claim, appeal or application` IS A NUMBER
    `Judges against it` IS A NUMBER

-- The sizes below 3 Judges that the Seventh and Tenth Schedules allow (ss 36(1), 54(1), 60E(1)).
@ref ss 36(1), 54(1), 60E(1); Seventh and Tenth Schedules
DECLARE `Smaller panel` IS ONE OF
    `a single Judge`
    `a single Judge or 2 Judges`
    `2 Judges`

-- One hearing of a case, with the facts that ss 8A(3), 10, 17B, 18H, 22A, 32, 36, 37, 50, 54 and 55 turn on.
@ref ss 8A, 10, 17B, 18H, 22A, 32, 36, 37, 50, 54, 55
DECLARE Hearing HAS
    bench IS A Bench
    `manner of hearing` IS A `Manner of hearing`
    `nature of the proceeding` IS A `Nature of the proceeding`
    `oral evidence is given in this part of the hearing` IS A BOOLEAN
    `all the parties consent` IS A BOOLEAN
    `a Judge is unable to continue` IS A BOOLEAN
    `how the Judges divided` IS A `Split of opinion`

§ `Core: people and officers`

-- The part a person plays in a case or a hearing, in the Act's words (ss 8(5), 13, 17C, 18M, 22, 29B, 38, 41, 73A-74).
@ref ss 8(5), 13, 17C, 18M, 22, 29B, 38, 41, 73A, 73B, 74
DECLARE Role IS ONE OF
    Claimant
    Defendant
    Appellant
    Respondent
    Applicant
    `Third party`
    `Judgment creditor`
    `Judgment debtor`
    Witness
    Accused
    `Attorney-General`
    `Public Prosecutor`
    `Advocate and solicitor`
    `Foreign lawyer or law expert registered under Part 4B of the Legal Profession Act 1966`
    Journalist
    `Member of the public`
    Assessor

-- A person in a case. Muslim status matters to s 17A and citizenship to s 15(1)(c).
@ref ss 15(1)(c), 17A
DECLARE Person HAS
    `reference for this person` IS A STRING
    roles IS A LIST OF Role
    `is a Muslim` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is a citizen of Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN

-- Officers of the Supreme Court (Part 6, ss 61-72, and ss 75-79). s 2 says "Registrar" includes the Deputy and Assistant Registrars.
@ref ss 2, 61-72, 75-79
DECLARE `Officer of the Supreme Court` IS ONE OF
    Registrar
    `Deputy Registrar`
    `Assistant Registrar`
    Sheriff
    `Deputy Sheriff`
    `Assistant Sheriff`
    Accountant
    `Commissioner for oaths`
    Interpreter
    Clerk
    `Process server`
    `Another officer of the Supreme Court`

§ `Core: the case, its subject, and the decisions made in it`

-- The subjects of case that the Act sorts by: ss 17(1), 17A(2), 18D(2), 29D, the Fifth and Sixth Schedules.
@ref ss 17, 17A, 18D, 28A, 29D; Fifth and Sixth Schedules
DECLARE `Subject of the case` IS ONE OF
    `divorce and matrimonial causes`
    `admiralty or shipping`
    bankruptcy
    companies
    `guardianship of infants`
    `guardianship of mentally disordered persons or persons of unsound mind`
    `probate or letters of administration`
    `recording a mediated settlement agreement under the Mediation Act 2017`
    `an international settlement agreement under the Singapore Convention on Mediation Act 2020`
    `maintenance for a wife or child`
    `custody of a child`
    `disposition or division of property on divorce`
    `constitutional or administrative law`
    `contempt of court`
    arbitration
    `international commercial arbitration`
    `insolvency, restructuring or dissolution of a corporation, limited liability partnership or sub-fund of a variable capital company`
    patents
    `Parliamentary elections`
    `Presidential elections`
    `land acquisition`
    `any other civil matter`

-- What an order or application is about, in the words of the Third, Fourth, Fifth, Seventh and Ninth Schedules.
@ref Third, Fourth, Fifth, Seventh and Ninth Schedules
DECLARE `Subject of an order or application` IS ONE OF
    `permission to defend proceedings`
    `setting aside a default judgment`
    `summary judgment`
    `striking out an action or a matter commenced by originating process`
    `striking out a pleading or part of a pleading`
    `dismissing an action or a matter commenced by originating process`
    `dismissing an action, or entering judgment, upon striking out or amendment of a pleading`
    `amending a pleading`
    `further and better particulars`
    interrogatories
    `discovery or inspection of documents`
    `security for costs`
    `a stay of proceedings`
    `whether the court has jurisdiction or should exercise it`
    `dealing with the action under the expedited procedure of the Rules of Court`
    `an interpleader summons`
    `costs or fees for hearing dates`
    `recording a judgment or order made by consent`
    `adducing further evidence`
    `an incidental direction or interim order under section 40(1) or 58(1)`
    `extending the time for filing and serving a notice of appeal`
    `permission to appeal`
    `a stay of execution or enforcement`
    `an interlocutory judgment`
    `a judgment or order after an assessment of damages`
    `a judgment or order after a taking of accounts between parties`
    `a judgment or order after an application for admission under section 15 of the Legal Profession Act 1966`
    `a judgment or order after proceedings other than the trial or hearing of an action or matter commenced by originating process`
    `another order or application`

-- How an order went. "Unconditional" and "on condition of payment into court or security" are the Third and Fifth Schedules' own two grants.
@ref Third and Fifth Schedules
DECLARE `Way an order went` IS ONE OF
    `granted unconditionally`
    `granted on condition that the party pays into court or gives security for the sum claimed`
    refused
    `made, being neither a grant nor a refusal`

@ref Third, Fourth, Fifth and Ninth Schedules
DECLARE Order HAS
    about IS A `Subject of an order or application`
    `way it went` IS A `Way an order went`

-- The facts about a decision that ss 21, 29, 29A, 29B, 46 and 47 and the Third, Fourth, Fifth, Sixth and Ninth Schedules turn on.
-- A decision with no amount or value in dispute carries NOTHING in the amount field: the Act does not say what an unvalued case counts as.
@ref ss 21, 29, 29A, 29B, 29C, 46, 47
DECLARE Decision HAS
    `made by` IS A Court
    `in the exercise of` IS A `Jurisdiction exercised`
    `nature of the proceeding` IS A `Nature of the proceeding`
    `subject of the case` IS A `Subject of the case`
    `the order` IS AN Order
    `made after the trial of an action` IS A BOOLEAN
    `made by consent of the parties` IS A BOOLEAN
    `amount in dispute or value of the subject matter, excluding interest and costs` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
    `date made` IS A DATE
    `date the judgment or order was extracted` IS A MAYBE DATE
    `a written law provides that it is final or that no appeal lies` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the parties agreed in writing, signed by or for each, that it is final` IS A BOOLEAN

§ `Core: the Act's own figures and dates`

-- Each figure is the Act's own, as printed in this text. The Minister or the President may substitute some of them by order (ss 21(3), 29A(3)):
-- a part takes the substituted figure as an input and uses these only as the figure the Act itself prints.
-- src:1733 | Magistrate’s Court (excluding interest and costs) does not
-- src:1734 | exceed $60,000 or such other amount as may be specified
@ref s 21(1)(a) (src:1733-1734)
`the Act's figure for s 21(1)(a)` MEANS 60000

-- src:2077 | (excluding interest and costs) does not exceed $250,000
@ref s 29A(1)(b) (src:2077)
`the Act's figure for s 29A(1)(b)` MEANS 250000

-- src:2139 | (b) the 15th day after the date on which the decision is made.
@ref s 29B(2)(b) (src:2139)
`the Act's number of days for s 29B(2)(b)` MEANS 15

-- src:2714 | (b) the expiration of 7 days after the date on which the decision
-- the same words in s 59(10)(b):
-- src:3283 | (b) the expiration of 7 days after the date on which the decision
@ref ss 41(10)(b) and 59(10)(b) (src:2714) (src:3283)
`the Act's number of days for ss 41(10)(b) and 59(10)(b)` MEANS 7

-- src:4627 | (a) remains in effect for a period (not exceeding 2 years) that is
-- the same words in s 73D(4)(a):
-- src:4724 | (a) remains in effect for a period (not exceeding 2 years) that is
@ref ss 73C(4)(a) and 73D(4)(a) (src:4627) (src:4724)
`the Act's number of years for ss 73C(4)(a) and 73D(4)(a)` MEANS 2

-- src:370 | [9 January 1970]
@ref commencement date printed under the long title (src:370)
`the day the Act was brought into operation` MEANS Date 9 1 1970

-- src:16 | Informal Consolidation – version in force from 1/7/2024
@ref consolidation banner (src:16)
`the day the text is in force from` MEANS Date 1 7 2024

-- src:1169 | that is registered under that section before 1 March 2009.
@ref s 17A(3A) (src:1169)
`the day before which a registered Muslim divorce counts under s 17A(3A)` MEANS Date 1 3 2009

-- src:1402 | 30 July 2020 —
@ref s 18D(2)(c) (src:1402)
`the day before which the Companies Act 1967 is read under s 18D(2)(c)` MEANS Date 30 7 2020
